 STANDING FACTS  (things that cost time to learn — do not re-derive)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

TOOLCHAIN
  * `dx` and the `dioxus` crate versions must match EXACTLY or dx refuses to run.
  * Workspace is edition 2024 -> `#[no_mangle]` must be `#[unsafe(no_mangle)]`.

BUILD / SERVE
  * `dx serve --hot-patch` is BROKEN on wasm (exceptions proposal). Use plain
    `dx serve --platform web`. Incremental rebuild is ~1.5s, which is enough.
  * `spikes/s1-hotreload` is EXCLUDED from the cargo workspace. You must `cd`
    into it; `--package s1-hotreload` from the root will not find it.

RUST / DIOXUS GOTCHAS
  * rsx! format strings interpolate IDENTS ONLY. `{a * b}` and `{if x {..}}`
    fail to parse. Compute into a `let` first.
  * `rsx!` and `EventHandler::new` need an active Dioxus SCOPE, not just a
    runtime. Use `VirtualDom::in_scope(ScopeId::ROOT, ..)`, not `in_runtime`.
    This covers ANYTHING that evaluates a story's props, not just rendering —
    `StoryDef::base_args()` panics outside a scope too.
  * `rsx!` expands to UNQUALIFIED `dioxus_core::` / `dioxus_signals::` paths.
    They resolve only because `dioxus::prelude` re-exports those crate names, so
    any file invoking rsx! — including one where OUR macro bridge expands it —
    needs `dioxus::prelude::*` in scope. `dioxus_storybook::prelude` re-exports
    it for this reason. Two globs re-exporting the same items do not conflict.
  * `Key` is at `dioxus::prelude::Key` (via dioxus_elements). There is no
    `dioxus::events::Key`.
  * Writing a `Signal` inside an `Fn` callback: copy it INSIDE the closure body
    (`let mut s = s;`). Capturing a `mut` copy makes the closure `FnMut`, even
    though `Signal` is `Copy`, because the binding is what gets mutated.
  * Rust privacy runs downward only. A build-script-generated registry module is
    an ANCESTOR of the story modules it references, so generated story statics
    must be `pub` regardless of the story fn's own visibility.

PROC MACROS vs RUST-ANALYZER  (cost a session to find; cargo test cannot catch it)
  * NEVER have one proc macro emit a `macro_rules!` that another proc macro's
    expansion then calls with a VALUE argument. rustc accepts it; rust-analyzer
    reports a false `E0425: no such value in this scope` at every call site.
    Closest upstream issue: rust-analyzer#10644, OPEN since 2021. Do not wait
    for a fix — emit a plain `fn` and have the macro learn the type it needs.
  * What DOES work across an expansion boundary: referencing a plain module
    ITEM (a `const`, a `fn`) generated by another proc macro. Verified.
  * This is NOT the common "proc macro library has no proc macros" error. That
    one means the dylib failed to load; the generic advice for it (enable
    procMacro, cargo clean, restart server) does not apply here. Ours loaded
    fine — derives expanded, only the macro_rules bridge failed.
  * `rust-analyzer diagnostics .` runs headless and is part of DEFINITION OF
    DONE for anything macro-facing. A green `cargo test` is not evidence that
    the authoring surface is usable in an editor.
  * RA also emits false `E0282: type annotations needed` for `Weak::upgrade`
    results and for tuple-destructuring closure params over `Rc<dyn Fn(..)>`.
    Write the type out; do not chase it.

PROJECT STRUCTURE
  * dx builds a workspace MEMBER fine — s1-hotreload's exclusion was not about
    dx disliking workspaces. `examples/button-gallery` is a member and builds.
  * `[profile.wasm-dev] panic = "abort"` is only honoured in the WORKSPACE ROOT
    manifest. In a member package Cargo ignores it.
  * `#[non_exhaustive]` has no effect inside its own crate — our own matches
    stay exhaustive, and a wildcard arm there is an unreachable-pattern warning.

TESTING TRAPS (both produced convincing WRONG answers — see LOG-0002)
  * LTO const-folds a static registry into a literal. A registry test that just
    counts entries can pass while proving nothing. Use `black_box` and read real
    string bytes out of linear memory.
  * macOS `sed -i ''` writes a sibling temp file (`.!PID!name.rs`). dx's file
    watcher latches onto THAT, not your file. Never use it to script an edit for
    a hot-reload measurement; write in place instead.


